lexical 6.1.1 (crates.io), replaced rather than forked.

Why: GHSA-c2hm-mjxv-89r4 (`lexical` <= 6.1.1) and GHSA-2326-pfpj-vx3h (`lexical-core` < 1.0.0) are soundness advisories in the crate's unsafe code (`Bytes::read`,
`BytesIter::read`, `write_float`, `radix`). The fixed releases are lexical 7 and lexical-core 1, but every published `swc_css_parser` (23 to 26) requires `lexical ^6.1`, so a
version bump of the parser does not reach them.

What: `swc_css_parser` calls only `lexical::parse` (three sites: the tokenizer's number, and the `An+B` offsets in the selector parser), on text its tokenizer has already
checked. This crate keeps that one function and its generic shape (`parse::<N, Bytes: AsRef<[u8]>>`), implemented with the standard library's `FromStr`. It is version 6.1.2 so
the parser's `^6.1` requirement is met, and the workspace `[patch.crates-io]` points `lexical` here. `lexical`, `lexical-core`, `lexical-parse-float`,
`lexical-parse-integer` and `lexical-util` are gone from Cargo.lock.

Differences from lexical 6, checked against the real 6.1.1 on 1.5 million random inputs (CSS number shapes up to 400 digits with sign, fraction and exponent for `f64`; signed
digit runs up to 12 digits for `i32`, the `An+B` offsets):
- `f64`: identical on every input, bit for bit, and on the hand-picked edges (`1.e3`, `-0`, `inf`, `nan`, empty, a lone sign or dot, separators, hex, whitespace, non-ASCII digits).
- `i32` within range: identical. Outside it (about 345 thousand of the inputs) lexical 6 either wrapped (`6255912584` gave 1960945288, 21 580 inputs) or returned an error
  (323 366 inputs), and `swc_css_parser` answers an error with `unreachable!`, a panic on a page's `:nth-child(9999999999n)`. The stand-in clamps to the type's range, as CSS does.
- Error values: one kind (`InvalidNumber`) instead of lexical's detailed ones; only their `Debug` is used. Digit separators and other radixes, which lexical can be configured to
  accept, are not (the tokenizer never produces them).

Re-check when `swc_css_parser` moves to a `lexical` release without the advisories: then this directory and the patch line can be deleted.
